{"title":"Hexagonal number","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Hexagonal numbers are a type of figurate number that represents the number of distinct dots needed to form a series of regular, overlapping hexagons. The sequence of standard hexagonal numbers (starting at 1) is: 1, 6, 15, 28, 45, 66, 91, 120, 153, 190...\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Hexagonal_number&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018090259\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.006 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.009 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 7/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 289/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 0/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.793      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.793      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36781:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018090259 and revision id 284180. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}